messages.content:
```json
{
  "respondents": 1500,
  "share_percent": {
    "Support": 42.0,
    "Oppose": 38.0,
    "Undecided": 20.0
  },
  "support_share_of_decided_percent": 52.5,
  "margin_over_oppose_in_respondents": 60
}
```

Two honest ways to frame the same numbers: support leads 42% to 38% of everyone, or 52.5% to 47.5% among the decided. The second framing quietly deletes the 300 undecided respondents, who are a fifth of the sample.

```json
{
  "yes_percent_by_group": {
    "18-29": 70.0,
    "30-49": 60.0,
    "50+": 45.8
  },
  "total_yes": 235,
  "total_no": 165,
  "overall_yes_percent": 58.8
}
```

Support falls steadily with age, from 70.0% among the youngest group to 45.8% among the oldest. The overall 58.8% is an average of groups that do not agree with each other.

```json
{
  "respondents": 1000,
  "exact_share_percent": {
    "Option A": 33.4,
    "Option B": 33.3,
    "Option C": 33.3
  },
  "whole_number_rounded_shares": {
    "Option A": 33,
    "Option B": 33,
    "Option C": 33
  },
  "sum_of_whole_number_shares": 99
}
```

Nothing is missing. Each true share is a third of the vote (33.3-33.4%), and rounding each one down to a whole number discards roughly a third of a percent three times. Rounded parts of a whole are not obliged to sum to exactly 100.

```json
{
  "clinic_a": {
    "patients": 1240,
    "readmissions": 62,
    "readmission_rate_percent": 5.0
  },
  "clinic_b": {
    "patients": 480,
    "readmissions": 36,
    "readmission_rate_percent": 7.5
  },
  "more_readmissions": "Clinic A",
  "higher_rate": "Clinic B"
}
```

A has more readmissions in raw count (62 vs 36) but the lower rate (5.0% vs 7.5%), simply because it treated about two and a half times as many patients. Comparing counts without the denominators would rank them backwards.

```json
{
  "store_1": {
    "visitors": 8400,
    "purchases": 672,
    "conversion_percent": 8.0
  },
  "store_2": {
    "visitors": 5100,
    "purchases": 510,
    "conversion_percent": 10.0
  },
  "more_purchases": "Store 1",
  "better_conversion": "Store 2"
}
```

Store 1 wins on volume, Store 2 wins on efficiency: one in ten of Store 2's visitors bought something, against one in twelve and a half at Store 1.

```json
{
  "district_a": {
    "doses": 45600,
    "population": 60000,
    "coverage_percent": 76.0
  },
  "district_b": {
    "doses": 52300,
    "population": 95000,
    "coverage_percent": 55.1
  },
  "more_doses": "District B",
  "better_coverage": "District A"
}
```

B delivered about 6,700 more doses yet covered a much smaller share of its people (55.1% vs 76.0%), because its population is over half again as large. Dose counts alone would crown the wrong district.

```json
{
  "factory_1": {
    "units": 24000,
    "defects": 96,
    "defect_rate_percent": 0.4
  },
  "factory_2": {
    "units": 9500,
    "defects": 57,
    "defect_rate_percent": 0.6
  },
  "more_defects": "Factory 1",
  "higher_defect_rate": "Factory 2"
}
```

Factory 1 scrapped more units in absolute terms, but proportionally Factory 2 is half again as defect-prone (0.60% vs 0.40%). Quality comparisons belong on the rate; waste-disposal planning belongs on the count.

```json
{
  "school_a": {
    "passed": 312,
    "sat": 390,
    "pass_rate_percent": 80.0
  },
  "school_b": {
    "passed": 198,
    "sat": 220,
    "pass_rate_percent": 90.0
  },
  "more_passes": "School A",
  "higher_pass_rate": "School B"
}
```

A produced 114 more passes than B, but nine of ten B candidates passed against eight of ten at A. A is the bigger school; B is, on this exam, the more successful one per student.

```json
{
  "variant_a": {
    "impressions": 31000,
    "clicks": 1240,
    "ctr_percent": 4.0
  },
  "variant_b": {
    "impressions": 34000,
    "clicks": 1530,
    "ctr_percent": 4.5
  },
  "more_clicks": "Variant B",
  "better_ctr": "Variant B",
  "ctr_gap_percentage_points": 0.5
}
```

B wins both ways here — more clicks and a better rate — but note the gap is 0.5 percentage points, which is a 12.5% relative improvement on A's 4.0%. The two phrasings describe the same difference.

```json
{
  "q1_total": 26300,
  "q2_total": 27524,
  "change_by_line": {
    "Alpha": {
      "usd": 1550,
      "percent": 12.5
    },
    "Beta": {
      "usd": -830,
      "percent": -10.0
    },
    "Gamma": {
      "usd": 504,
      "percent": 9.0
    }
  },
  "total_change_percent": 4.7,
  "only_declining_line": "Beta"
}
```

Headline growth of 4.7% hides the mix: Alpha and Gamma grew by double and near-double digits while Beta shrank 10.0%. The total rose because the growing lines are larger, not because everything grew.

```json
{
  "total_2024": 930,
  "total_2025": 1021,
  "change_by_category": {
    "Individual": {
      "count": 56,
      "percent": 13.6
    },
    "Family": {
      "count": 15,
      "percent": 6.4
    },
    "Student": {
      "count": 26,
      "percent": 13.8
    },
    "Senior": {
      "count": -6,
      "percent": -6.4
    }
  },
  "total_change_percent": 9.8,
  "only_declining_category": "Senior"
}
```

Overall growth of 9.8% is broad but not universal: three categories grew while Senior membership quietly fell 6.4% — small in count (6 people), easy to miss inside a healthy headline.

```json
{
  "campaign_a": {
    "sent": 12000,
    "open_rate_percent": 26.0,
    "click_rate_of_sent_percent": 3.9,
    "clicks_per_open_percent": 15.0
  },
  "campaign_b": {
    "sent": 9500,
    "open_rate_percent": 28.0,
    "click_rate_of_sent_percent": 3.6,
    "clicks_per_open_percent": 12.9
  },
  "better_open_rate": "Campaign B",
  "better_click_rate_of_sent": "Campaign A"
}
```

The campaigns split the honors: B's subject line worked better (28.0% opens vs 26.0%), but A's content converted better — 15.0% of its openers clicked, against 12.9% for B, giving A the higher click rate on sent despite fewer opens per send.

```json
{
  "north": {
    "stores": [
      142,
      167,
      129
    ],
    "stated_subtotal": 448,
    "actual_subtotal": 438,
    "ok": false
  },
  "south": {
    "stores": [
      98,
      121,
      116
    ],
    "stated_subtotal": 335,
    "actual_subtotal": 335,
    "ok": true
  },
  "east": {
    "stores": [
      154,
      139,
      147
    ],
    "stated_subtotal": 440,
    "actual_subtotal": 440,
    "ok": true
  },
  "corrected_grand_total": 1213
}
```

Two of the three subtotals are exact. North's is overstated by 10 (448 claimed vs 438 actual), which also inflates any grand total built from the stated subtotals: the corrected company figure is 1,213 units, not 1,223.
